AB  - The intake of plant‐based yoghurt is on the rise due to consumers' increasing awareness of its rich nutrient composition and health‐promoting properties. However, liquid plant‐based yoghurt is susceptible to microbial and chemical spoilage, reducing its shelf life and nutritional and health‐promoting qualities. This study evaluated the nutritional, antioxidant, anti‐inflammatory, and some metabolic syndrome‐related enzyme‐inhibitory properties of dehydrated bambara groundnut yoghurt (BGN‐Y) supplemented with cocoa and turmeric powder. Dehydrated BGN‐Y (control, unsupplemented), cocoa‐supplemented (BGN‐Y + C), turmeric‐supplemented (BGN‐Y + T), and cocoa‐turmeric‐supplemented (BGN‐Y + C + T) samples were analyzed for proximate composition, bioactive constituents, antioxidant, anti‐inflammatory, and enzyme‐inhibitory activities, and sensory attributes using standard analytical methods. Supplementation with cocoa and turmeric decreased ( p < 0.05) the crude protein and fat contents but increased the available carbohydrate of the yoghurt. It increased ( p < 0.05) the yoghurt's bioactive constituents, with BGN‐Y + C recording the highest total flavonoid, total phenolic, tannins, and total saponin contents. Similarly, antioxidant [ABTS* + ‐, DPPH*‐, and nitric oxide (NO*)‐scavenging capacity, ferric‐reducing power] and anti‐inflammatory (membrane stabilization and protein denaturation inhibition) activities of the yoghurt samples were enhanced due to cocoa and turmeric supplementation. BGN‐Y + C exhibited the strongest antioxidant activity, with scavenging concentrations of 99.02 ± 0.90 μg/mL (ABTS* + ), 89.77 ± 2.68 μg/mL (DPPH*), 105.39 ± 6.04 μg/mL (NO*), and 1.31 ± 0.10 mmol Fe 2+ /g (ferric‐reducing power). The supplemented BGN‐Y also demonstrated improved α‐amylase‐, α‐glucosidase‐, and ACE‐inhibitory activities. The samples were sensorially acceptable, with BGN‐Y + C recording the highest ( p < 0.05) acceptability. These findings demonstrate that cocoa and turmeric supplementation enhanced the antioxidant, anti‐inflammatory, and metabolic syndrome‐related enzyme‐inhibitory qualities of dehydrated BGN‐Y, suggesting its potential as a functional food product.
