AB  - Commodity prices continue to be difficult to forecast accurately because of their intrinsic volatility, nonlinear dynamics, and sensitivity to exogenous shocks. To assist in identifying effective techniques for forecasting commodity prices, particularly cocoa prices, this study develops a unified benchmarking framework to assess the predictive performance of various modelling approaches. These modelling approaches include classical statistical methods (ARIMA), regularised linear models (Ridge and Elastic Net), tree-based ensemble techniques (Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM), and deep learning architectures (LSTM and TCN). The study uses daily cocoa price data from 2005 to 2026 and employs log transformation for variance stabilization. Thorough feature engineering, including lag structures, rolling statistics, and return-based features, follows. The study uses bias-corrected back-transformation to guarantee consistent and economically significant evaluation of the models on the original pricing scale. Standard metrics (RMSE, MAE, R2, and MAPE) and walk-forward validation are used to evaluate the model's performance. According to empirical findings, regularised linear models, in particular Ridge regression, outperform all other methods, achieving the highest levels of accuracy and explanatory power. ARIMA and tree-based approaches fall well short of the impressive performance of deep learning models. The results emphasise the value of appropriate transformation handling for predicting evaluation and demonstrate the efficacy of simpler regularised models for handling noisy, high-dimensional financial data.
